"\n\n<p>Philadelphia in 1787 gave America a framework but no operating manual. The Constitution specified how the three branches would be formed and how they would check each other, but it said almost nothing about how a president should take office, how he should conduct daily business with Congress, whether he should assemble his department heads as a council, how a fiscal state should be built from nothing, or whether he could announce the nation's position in a sudden foreign crisis before Congress had acted. These questions were blanks on paper. Many things later taken for granted as natural features of American government were, during those eight years from 1789 to 1797, nothing more than unsolidified practices. They acquired institutional weight because Washington filled the blanks in a particular way, and the way he filled them became the precedents that successors followed.</p>\n\n<p>The most stable judgment about Washington's presidency is not that he was an indispensable great man, but something more specific: his historical significance lay in converting a designed republic into a functioning republic. And that conversion was accompanied, from its very first day, by tensions the institution could not eliminate — only contain.</p>\n\n<p>One fundamental feature of this construct distinguishes it from many that came before. Madison's generation, the designers of the machine, placed no confidence in the virtue of rulers. They assumed that those who held power would make mistakes, be driven by interest and passion, and try to overstep their boundaries. So they used separation of powers, checks and balances, and the dual structure of federal and state authority to accommodate conflict and dissent within the institution itself rather than trying to eliminate them. This construct, in other words, abandoned closure from the start. And it did not merely fail to suppress disagreement — it actively acknowledged the legitimacy of disagreement and faction, giving conflict legal channels within its structure. It did not pursue a unified will without discord; it prepared to let contradictions persist, be absorbed by structure, and continue evolving within structure. That is why the remainders this construct generates need not tear it apart — they are continuously taken in and carried forward. It is also why this series can run for two centuries without interruption.</p>\n\n<p>Washington stood at exactly the position where this feature would face its first test. He may not have fully accepted the design. He detested factions and longed for a republican unity that stood above strife. But the machine he was asked to operate had a logic that did not align with his personal aspirations. In some sense, the story of these eight years is the story of how a construct that did not seek closure began to run under a man who longed for closure — and how, in the end, the construct's logic prevailed.</p>\n\n<h3>1. Precedents: Completing the Framework into an Institution</h3>\n\n<p>On April 30, 1789, Washington took the oath of office at Federal Hall in New York. Afterward he entered the Senate chamber and delivered the first inaugural address to both houses of Congress. This act was itself a precedent. The Constitution did not require an inaugural address, but after Washington did it, it became a practice that every successor followed. A gesture with no explicit constitutional basis became, from that moment, an assumed part of the office.</p>\n\n<p>Washington was acutely conscious that he was making precedents. Shortly before taking office, he wrote to Henry Knox comparing his feelings about accepting the presidency to those of a person condemned to death as the moment of execution approaches. The following year he wrote the famous line that he walked on untrodden ground, and that almost every step of his conduct might be drawn into precedent. This was not rhetorical modesty — it was an accurate description of his situation. The presidency had no instruction manual; he was the one writing it.</p>\n\n<p>The cabinet presented another gap between text and practice. The Constitution did not create any collective body called a cabinet. Congress established the War Department, the Treasury, and a Department of Foreign Affairs (soon reorganized as the State Department) in summer and fall of 1789, and the Judiciary Act created the Attorney General. By autumn Washington had settled the core of what would be called his first cabinet: Hamilton at Treasury, Jefferson at State, Knox at War, Randolph as Attorney General. But the cabinet did not spring into existence fully formed. Historical research indicates that for the first two-plus years, Washington relied primarily on written opinions and individual consultations. The first recognized full cabinet meeting came only in late 1791. The cabinet, in other words, was not produced directly by constitutional text — it was an administrative form gradually invented under the pressure of governance. That a central institution of executive operations was still uncongealed even in its most basic form: that was the true state of this construct at its opening.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Fiscal State: Putting the Construct into Combat</h3>\n\n<p>Hamilton's financial program was what first drove this construct into real action. In early 1790, Hamilton submitted to Congress his Report on Public Credit, proposing to treat the management of the national debt left by the Revolutionary War as the foundation of the new nation's creditworthiness. His core principle was to maintain the debt at face value, have the federal government assume state debts, and make public credit an instrument of state capacity.</p>\n\n<p>The proposal had two political flashpoints. Assumption of state debts was opposed by southern states that had largely paid down their own obligations and did not want to subsidize others. The question of who should be paid — whether to honor present holders of securities at face value without distinguishing original holders from speculators who had bought cheap — divided Madison and Hamilton. Madison argued for some compensatory distinction in favor of original holders; Hamilton insisted on honoring contracts as they stood, arguing that the nation's credit would be destroyed if the rights of current holders were compromised.</p>\n\n<p>The impasse over assumption was broken through a political exchange known in traditional accounts as the Dinner Table Compromise. Jefferson hosted Hamilton and Madison at dinner, facilitating a trade: the capital would be placed on the Potomac, and assumption would pass. The Residence Act followed in July, and the Funding Act in August. Whether the dinner was a literal sealed deal or a somewhat less formal alignment of interests remains debated — the account rests largely on Jefferson's later recollections — but the basic outline of an exchange has been accepted by most historians. The fact that even the location of the capital was determined by trade rather than by some predetermined plan is itself worth noting.</p>\n\n<p>The bank controversy followed immediately. Hamilton's Report on a National Bank came at the end of 1790; Congress passed the bill for the First Bank of the United States; Washington signed it into law on February 25, 1791. The debate sharpened the question of constitutional interpretation for the first time. Hamilton argued Congress could exercise implied powers under the Necessary and Proper Clause. Jefferson and Madison insisted on a stricter reading of enumerated powers. Washington accepted Hamilton's argument and did not veto the bill — opening a crucially important precedent for broad interpretation of federal power.</p>\n\n<p>A construct that abandons closure and prepares to accommodate conflict long-term does not thereby escape the need for strength. On the contrary, it needs more genuine state capacity than a construct that achieves unity through coercion, because it must be capable of acting even as disagreement persists — capable of repaying debts, capable of being taken seriously as a partner in the international system. The Confederation had been nearly paralyzed fiscally. The fiscal state built during Washington's presidency, whatever one thinks about its distribution of benefits, gave this construct for the first time the material basis to convert decisions into action. The accumulation of capacity was the material precondition for a construct that did not seek closure to keep absorbing its remainders across more than two centuries.</p>\n\n<h3>3. The Remainder That Was Acknowledged: Party Strife</h3>\n\n<p>Washington hated political parties. By the time of the Farewell Address in 1796, this was stated with great clarity. But the operation of Washington's own government put all the conditions necessary for party formation on display.</p>\n\n<p>Hamilton's approach was to build a strong federal state with public credibility, debt-paying capacity, and a position in the international system. Jefferson and Madison grew increasingly worried that such a state would concentrate fiscal, administrative, and social influence in a small number of hands, pulling the republic away from the political virtue they associated with local self-governance and independent farmers. The Hamilton-Jefferson conflict cannot be written as a personality clash; it must be written as a dispute over how the state should be constructed. By 1792 and 1793, pro-administration and anti-administration networks had formed stable alignments in newspapers, in congressional voting, in state-level organizations. Jefferson and Madison built the anti-administration network that became the Democratic-Republicans; Hamilton used financial and press networks to consolidate the Federalists.</p>\n\n<p>The true remainder that could not be eliminated was not the party organizations themselves but partisan strife — the political conflict generated by fundamental disagreement. Parties were simply the organized, visible form of that conflict. What Washington warned against was the spirit of party, the strife itself. In a competitive representative system, wherever major disagreements about national direction persist long enough, strife will generate and eventually organize into parties. This was a highly predictable outcome. Washington's inability to prevent it does not mark a personal failure; it marks the fact that institutions do not automatically dissolve deep disagreements into consensus.</p>\n\n<p>This is precisely what distinguishes this construct from an order that pursues closure. A closing construct treats dissent as a disease to be eradicated, using every available method to suppress it and achieve unified will. This construct does not do that. It acknowledges the legitimacy of dissent, gives it the channels of separation of powers, checks and balances, press, and elections — letting it contend within structure. The faction-free republican unity that Washington longed for was essentially a closing aspiration, a desire for a unified national will. The construct he was operating was designed not to depend on or pursue such unity. When the two met, the logic of the construct prevailed: strife generated as predicted, parties appeared as predicted, and the institution absorbed them without breaking.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Foreign Crisis and the Question of Executive Power</h3>\n\n<p>If party strife was a remainder generated from within, foreign crisis forced out a question from another direction that would prove equally durable: how much initiative does the president hold in foreign affairs?</p>\n\n<p>When war renewed in Europe in 1793, Washington issued a Proclamation of Neutrality on April 22. The key was not merely that America declared itself neutral but that the president announced the nation's foreign posture before Congress had acted or legislated on the matter. This was the true origin point of the debate about executive foreign power.</p>\n\n<p>Hamilton, writing as Pacificus, defended the proclamation by arguing that the Constitution vested the general executive power in the president, and that outside the exceptions expressly reserved to Congress, the president could take the initiative in determining the nation's foreign obligations. Madison, writing as Helvidius, countered that powers related to war and peace belonged fundamentally to the legislature, and that proclaiming neutrality was effectively deciding the nation's foreign legal status without congressional authorization. Their exchange was the first complete airing of whether the president holds broad foreign initiative — and that question would never close. It would reappear under Lincoln, under Roosevelt, in the Cold War national security apparatus, under Nixon, in forms suited to each era. The Neutrality Proclamation did not close the question; it opened it.</p>\n\n<p>The Jay Treaty extended this pattern. Signed in November 1794, approved by the Senate in June 1795 with exactly the two-thirds threshold on a twenty-to-ten vote, signed by Washington in August, and formally published in February 1796, the treaty was deeply unpopular. Washington judged that it bought what the young nation most needed: avoidance of war with Britain and time to consolidate, recover, and rearm. For opponents it was capitulation to British commercial interests. For supporters it was realism for a weak state in a dangerous international system. Together the Neutrality Proclamation, the Genet Affair, and the Jay Treaty established a continuous institutional history rather than three isolated foreign-policy episodes. What they collectively produced was not a settled answer about executive power but an open zone of contest that would be fought over again and again.</p>\n\n<h3>5. Can the Federal Government Enforce Its Own Laws?</h3>\n\n<p>If foreign crisis tested the president's outward reach, the Whiskey Rebellion of 1794 tested a simpler domestic question: could federal law actually be enforced on federal territory?</p>\n\n<p>The excise tax on distilled spirits passed in 1791 was bitterly resented in western Pennsylvania. Whiskey was not merely a commodity there; it was the practical means of converting grain into transportable value in a region short of cash, short of roads, and thinly connected to eastern markets. The tax fell disproportionately on frontier communities, resistance escalated, and tax collectors faced harassment and violence. In the summer of 1794, following the attack on tax inspector John Neville's home and the gathering of thousands of insurgents on Braddock's Field, Washington moved through the procedures established in the Militia Act of 1792, issued proclamations, dispatched peace commissioners, and when negotiations failed, called up militia from Pennsylvania, Maryland, Virginia, and New Jersey — a force that contemporary and official sources put at roughly thirteen thousand men. Washington accompanied the force westward to Bedford, Pennsylvania, before handing over the active campaign to Henry Lee.</p>\n\n<p>The result was decisive in its symbolism: the force of the federal government's presence dissolved the resistance. About a hundred and fifty were arrested; most were released for insufficient evidence; two were convicted of treason and both were pardoned by Washington. The importance lay not in bloodshed but in demonstration: the federal government could not only legislate, it could execute. At the same time, the roughness of arrests and procedures left its mark in local memory. The establishment of capacity and the generation of grievance were, as they often are, two faces of the same act.</p>\n\n<h3>6. The Deepest Remainder: Slavery</h3>\n\n<p>Beyond party, executive power, and enforcement, Washington's presidency was connected to a remainder deeper than all of them — one belonging not to the question of how power is distributed but to the question of who counts as a member of the community at all. That remainder was slavery.</p>\n\n<p>The new republic founded its language on liberty while excluding a substantial portion of the people living within its borders from that language. Washington was the most direct embodiment of this contradiction. At his death in 1799, Mount Vernon held 317 enslaved people, of whom only 123 were legally his to dispose of by will — the others were dower slaves controlled through his wife's inheritance. Washington's will stipulated that those he owned be freed upon his wife's death, a significant and unusual provision, but one covering less than half the enslaved population of the estate. Broader estimates suggest he owned or controlled something on the order of 670 enslaved people over his lifetime.</p>\n\n<p>Washington did express private discomfort with slavery and private hope for gradual abolition. His correspondence indicates that in the 1780s and 1790s he repeatedly said he wished to be rid of slaveholding, that he opposed separating enslaved families, and that he favored legislative action toward gradual abolition. Yet as president he raised no public antislavery banner, and during his Philadelphia residence he secretly rotated enslaved household servants to evade Pennsylvania's gradual abolition law, which would have granted freedom to any enslaved person resident in the state for six months. Ona Judge, one of those servants, escaped in 1796 under exactly these circumstances.</p>\n\n<p>The deepest remainder was thus acknowledged at the founding moment, deferred, but not resolved. It would resurface in the Missouri debates, come close to tearing this construct apart in the fire of the Civil War, and force the construct to rewrite its most fundamental rules more than half a century later. A construct that does not seek closure shows here its heaviest face: it can accommodate a contradiction that even its founders found troubling, let that contradiction persist for generations, until later people have the strength to confront it.</p>\n\n<h3>7. Farewell and Retirement: The Construct's First Self-Limitation</h3>\n\n<p>The Farewell Address of 1796 should be defined precisely in genre: not a speech to Congress but a public letter, published in a Philadelphia newspaper on September 19. Its composition was not sudden. As early as 1792, Washington had considered not seeking a second term, and Madison had drafted an early version. By 1796, Hamilton did the major rewriting, though Washington remained involved throughout and produced the final text. Calling it ghostwritten by Hamilton is too crude; the accurate description is that the ideas were Washington's, Hamilton supplied substantial literary labor, and Madison's 1792 draft was an important antecedent.</p>\n\n<p>On parties, what Washington warned against was the spirit of party, and he particularly highlighted geographical discriminations — regional factions. He did not predict every subsequent form of party strife, nor did he propose eliminating all political groupings. He identified partisan competition as a non-negligible danger to republican government, especially insofar as party divisions gave openings to foreign influence.</p>\n\n<p>On foreign policy, the Farewell Address is most commonly misquoted as advocating against all alliances. Washington actually wrote of avoiding permanent alliances, while explicitly allowing for temporary alliances for extraordinary emergencies. His objection was to long-term fixed political and military entanglements, not to all temporary cooperation. Writing this as Washington proposing that America never form alliances is obvious oversimplification.</p>\n\n<p>But the address's true institutional weight lay not only in what it said — it lay in what it accompanied: Washington chose not to seek a third term. He was not compelled by law; many wanted him to stay. Precisely because of this, the choice carried unmistakable symbolic meaning. It established that the presidency did not exist for any indispensable individual — that the office should be capable of continuing after any particular person's departure.</p>\n\n<p>This was the construct's first self-limitation, and it was a decisive proof that the construct could run forward. The remainders it had generated — party strife, the boundaries of executive power, the costs of enforcement, and the deepest remainder of slavery — had not been eliminated, only accommodated. Now, at the moment of departure, the construct had to answer one final question: after the person who had converted personal prestige into institutional legitimacy departed, could the construct still stand? Washington answered by stepping down. He demonstrated that this construct did not depend on any particular person, did not need a permanent center, did not need a unified will — that it could continue to function on the basis of accommodated conflict and rotating power.</p>\n\n      "
